### How to build your trees
- **No worktree is created in the NexusAI repo, and you never work in its `2_Project_Files` checkout or any builder `worktrees/` directory.** In that repo use ONLY read verbs: `show`, `diff`, `log`, `ls-tree`, `cat-file`, `grep`, `ls-remote`, `rev-parse`, `merge-base`, `rev-list` (plus `count-objects` for §7.9). **NEVER `fetch`, `pull`, `push`, `checkout`, `worktree`, `commit`, `stash`, `gc`, `merge`; and `merge-tree --write-tree` ONLY with `GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY=<your own scratch dir> GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES=<repo>/.git/objects`, or in your own `--shared` clone.**
- **Every tree is K1** — `git clone --shared --no-checkout <repo> <dir>` then `checkout` in YOUR clone (origin removed; local identity; `gc.auto 0`; `core.fsmonitor false`; hooks off), under a fresh `mktemp -d` in `/Volumes/KK_T9_External_HDD/!CODING/Testing Agent MAIN/projects/nexusai/qa-trees/batch20.XXXXXX/`. Status-check before use.
- **Each tree is EXCLUSIVE to this gate and to ONE purpose.** A fresh arm per mutant; never reuse a mutant tree for a clean arm; `batch20`-prefixed directories only; never run in, write to, or clone node_modules FROM another gate's trees (gate 19's `batch19.*` included).
- **The red-at-parent trees (each committed IN YOUR CLONE ONLY, hashed after the change):** **A:** head tree with `azureLogAnalytics.js` → `bd0a382`, `lawEndpointPolicy.js` → `beaa819`, `syntheticFeed.js` → `884cc39` (round 1's product — the shape cells red); a second arm with ONLY `syntheticFeed.js` → `884cc39` (the synthetic cell red); a third with `9e378bc`'s product (the wrapper check — synthetic-feed red); **B:** head tree with ONLY `jsonStorage.js` → `2abb5cf`; **C:** head tree with `static/legal.html` → `9567a81` and `static/js/legal.js` removed; **D:** head tree with `entraProvisioning.js` → `ba1dcfe` and `scimProvisioning.js` → `2dec10c` (`logSafe.js` left); **E:** the head tree with each F-3 shape planted in `server.js` (the arm must redden) and the head unmutated (green).
- **`node_modules` — TWO locks (T2).** Each tree runs on node_modules PROVEN for its lock (`npm ls --all --offline` rc 0; every `invalid`/`extraneous`/`missing` line quoted; proxy-addr, sqlite3, playwright read back FROM THE TREE'S OWN PATH). **Source (H-31):** ONCE PER LOCK, `npm_config_update_notifier=false npm ci --offline --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund --cache "<your mktemp dir>/npm-cache"` in YOUR OWN K1 tree for that lock, the `_cacache` an APFS clone (`cp -c -R`) of `~/.npm/_cacache`; then sqlite3's binding from an APFS clone of `~/.npm/_prebuilds` by sqlite3's own `prebuild-install` inside the strict belt, with a load proof (ruling m). An `ENOTCACHED` is NOT RUN for that tree — name it and mail a QUESTION; **never go online.** Every other tree of the same lock gets node_modules by an APFS clone of YOUR proven tree. A real directory, never a symlink. **Never `npm install`.** Say that `--ignore-scripts` differs from CI's form (L-Y1). **Merge clones need node_modules too.**

## 2. Why these tiers, and who is waiting — severity rules
- **RD-761 fix round TIER 1:** a non-GUID workspace id that reaches a token request, a URL string sent anywhere, or an outbound request at either sink is a **Blocker** (#256-#259's class); a CALLER-named host reached is a **Blocker**; the workspace value reaching a log line or a response body is a **Major**; the refusal renamed by the breaker, or the breaker no longer opening on real primary failures, is a **Major**; the synthetic feed no longer serving, or its table discovery answering anything but the pre-fix sentence, is a **Major** (the demo); a GUID workspace (either case) refused is a **Major**; PR #58's analysis not clearing #256-#259, or any NEW alert at either C-190 threshold, is a **Blocker for landing** (T8); a declared mutant not red as claimed is a **Minor**; a C-68 cell that passed at M0 failing on MT-A/MTF is a **Major**; a READY claim that is false (WRONG 1-4) is a **Minor**.
- **RD-747 TIER 1:** sign-in keys lost on any arm where they were the last content written (S1's class, b3) is a **Blocker**; a cleared key coming back after a restart, tear, delete or corruption (r9a-c, I1, E1, E2's class) is a **Major** (the ticket's claim); torn/empty/unparseable bytes read as a clear (K6, S2's class) is a **Major**; RD-424/RD-404/RD-408 restores no longer happening (K1-K3) is a **Major**; RD-407's refusal past the sign-in keys changed (K4) is a **Blocker**; the erasure or export reading recovery copies differently is a **Major**; a declared mutant not red as claimed is a **Minor**; a C-68 cell failing on MT-B/MTF is a **Major**.
- **RD-831 TIER 1:** either pane not showing exactly the served text under the default CSP on the head, in open mode or signed in, is a **Major** (C-14: the Terms are the licence); a CSP violation on the head's page from its own scripts is a **Major**; `legal.js` not byte-equal to the removed loader (beyond the de-indent and the comment) is a **Major** (*"moved VERBATIM"*); a gated page body served anonymously, or any new anonymous surface beyond `js/legal.js` (an inert loader), is a **Blocker**; the fallback text not shown honestly on a failing route (L2) is a **Minor**; a declared mutant not red as claimed is a **Minor**; a C-68 cell failing on MT-C/MTF is a **Major**.
- **RD-802 TIER 2:** a request value that can still start a new line (CR, LF, U+2028/2029) in either SCIM line is a **Major**; an ordinary value whose log text changed is a **Minor**; the 428 refusal on a non-integer version moved or weakened is a **Major**; a C-68 cell failing on MT-D/MTF is a **Major**; RD-802 landing without RD-801 is a **Major** (T5).
- **RD-795 TIER 2 (ROUND 2 OF 2):** a shape carrying the request's own count that keeps R8 green, beyond the declared ones, is a **Minor** (C-40; the residue is ticketed under the cap); a gate-16 survivor that no longer reddens R8 is a **Major** (the round's claim); R8 not running (renamed, skipped, filtered empty — a check that cannot fail) is a **Major**; a C-68 cell failing on MT-E/MTF is a **Major**.
- **Who is waiting:** RD-761's merge author is **NexusAI-R (S92R)**; RD-747's **NexusAI-N (S87N)**; RD-831's **NexusAI-P (S91P)**; RD-802's **NexusAI-O (S93O, successor of S87O)** after RD-801/822; RD-795's **NexusAI-M (S86M)**. **Class cap:** RD-795 is round 2 of 2.

